  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of employers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Small Island Developing States (SIDS) in the Caribbean and Pacific are highly vulnerable to climate-change-related disasters, experiencing severe storms, floods, and other natural disasters that significantly impact businesses and economies. These disasters can disrupt operations, destroy assets, and cause economic losses, affecting various sectors and supply chains. The private sector plays a crucial role in resilience building and disaster risk reduction, partnering with the public sector to integrate disaster risk management into business models, raise awareness, train employees, conduct research, innovate, develop technology, and share knowledge and practices.

The inter-regional collaboration programme for 2024/2025 aims to promote business resiliency among Caribbean and Pacific SIDS, building on research conducted in 2023 on public-private partnerships for resilience. This initiative will address challenges such as climate change, skills drain, weak governance, poor access to finance, inflation, and high crime. Beneficiaries include businesses and communities in SIDS, with the work being done in collaboration with Employers&apos; and Business Membership Organizations (EBMOs), public sector partners, and other stakeholders. The ILO will support strategy development, policy engagement, and services to enhance business resiliency in these regions.</narrative>
  </description>
